1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:03,560
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:04,720
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:05,720
My name is Katherine.

4
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:10,000
And Marco, today we're talking about a very important part of daily life.

5
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:11,440
That's right.

6
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:21,150
Today we're going to take a look at a lesson about technology and something that has become so popular and part of our everyday lives that it's necessary for us to acknowledge it in English.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:21,930
That's right.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,550
So we're talking about cellular phones, but not just cellular phones.

9
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,890
Something you can do and that many people often do on their cellular phones.

10
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:30,630
That's right.

11
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,110
So before we get into that, let's listen to the dialogue, try to understand what it's all about and we'll be back in a bit.

12
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:43,350
Why didn't you text me last night?

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:43,970
What?

14
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:45,770
I sent you three or four messages.

15
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:47,330
I didn't get any of them.

16
00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:50,770
I was waiting for you to text me the address of where the party was and I never got your message.

17
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:52,190
Why didn't you just call?

18
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,250
I hate sending SMS messages.

19
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:56,710
Well, because I didn't have any credit on my phone.

20
00:00:56,890 --> 00:00:57,750
I used it all up this month.

21
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:00,490
I thought you had an unlimited SMS plan.

22
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:04,230
I do, but if I don't have any credit in my phone, it won't let me call or send messages.

23
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:06,090
No wonder you didn't get my texts.

24
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:09,680
All right, we're back.

25
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:11,900
So now let's take a look at Language Takeaway.

26
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:15,160
Language Takeaway.

27
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:22,520
First up on Language Takeaway, we have the verb that is the focus of today's dialogue, to text.

28
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:24,480
That's right.

29
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:28,000
To text is a verb in this case, to text.

30
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Text me.

31
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,360
Why didn't you text me?

32
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:31,640
Why didn't you text me?

33
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,500
Now remember that text means other things in other situations.

34
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:37,160
Here it's a verb.

35
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:43,420
It's a very new word as a verb from the last maybe 15 years or so with cell phones.

36
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:51,120
So that's actually what it means is to send a text message via cell phone.

37
00:01:51,320 --> 00:01:52,160
So you send a message.

38
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:53,480
That's right.

39
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:55,320
You actually write the message, right?

40
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:55,820
It's not a call.

41
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:57,440
When I call you, I talk to you.

42
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:00,820
When you text someone, you actually write a message.

43
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:05,280
It's very short and then you send it and the other person can read it.

44
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,280
Right.

45
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:09,800
So instead of saying, why didn't you send me a text message last night?

46
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,920
We just say, why didn't you text me?

47
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:12,220
All right.

48
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:19,180
Now, another word that we often hear about texts is the noun messages or message.

49
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,380
So I sent you three or four messages.

50
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:23,320
That's right.

51
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,860
So when we're talking about texting someone, what we're doing is sending a message.

52
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,080
A text message.

53
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:30,060
A text message.

54
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,360
That's right.

55
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,680
Because we can send a voice message.

56
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:34,280
That's right.

57
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:36,520
So voice is me talking, hi Marco, it's Catherine.

58
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:37,000
How are you doing?

59
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:37,340
Right.

60
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:39,640
But a text message is actually words.

61
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:41,280
I write them out.

62
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:42,280
That's right.

63
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:42,420
That's right.

64
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:45,880
So, but then we could actually use the verb form as well.

65
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:49,650
So we can say to text or we can say to message.

66
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:51,730
Why didn't you message me last night?

67
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:52,150
That's right.

68
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:57,310
So we use this slang to message someone because text is very common and everyone uses it.

69
00:02:57,690 --> 00:03:00,910
But normally young people say, hey, why didn't you message me?

70
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,720
Or aren't you ever going to message me?

71
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:04,720
Right, right, right.

72
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,240
Or stop messaging in class.

73
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:07,240
That's right.

74
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,900
So these are verbs to text and to message.

75
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:10,060
That's right.

76
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:19,400
Now, if we see down towards the middle of the dialogue, we see that the person hates sending SMS messages.

77
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:29,520
So it's kind of explaining already what's been said because you have SMS messages and this abbreviation stands for?

78
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:30,260
Short message service.

79
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:35,460
Short S, message M, service S. So short message service.

80
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,100
But most people around the world say SMS.

81
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:38,760
Right.

82
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:49,840
So they say, oh, send me an SMS or send me an SMS message, which again, like we see in the dialogue, it's kind of contradictory because we're already saying short message service message.

83
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:51,940
Yeah, but most people don't know what SMS means.

84
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:59,210
So they just say, hey, send me an SMS message or send me a text message or I didn't get your SMS.

85
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:00,390
That's right.

86
00:04:00,890 --> 00:04:06,890
Now, lastly, we didn't get the SMS because we didn't have any credit on the phone.

87
00:04:07,390 --> 00:04:07,670
All right.

88
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:10,350
Now, credit is an important word here.

89
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:10,890
It's a noun.

90
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:11,770
It's a thing.

91
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:15,380
We have credit or we don't have credit.

92
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:18,290
And it's important to remember that credit stands for money.

93
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:19,650
All right.

94
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,900
So maybe I buy $10 of credit for my cell phone and that means I can send many, many messages.

95
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:32,400
But when I have zero credit, I can't send any messages.

96
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:33,060
That's right.

97
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:42,680
So this is important because usually in many places you have to have this amount of money in your phone in order to be able to talk and send messages.

98
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:45,420
So this money that you have is called credit.

99
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:48,760
So you can say, I don't have any credit on my phone.

100
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:49,460
That's right.

101
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:54,320
And it's basically a way of having money without having real money.

102
00:04:54,620 --> 00:05:04,400
You actually buy credit from your cell phone provider like AT&T or if you have China Telecom, that's who you buy credit from.

103
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:05,020
That's right.

104
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:05,980
Very good.

105
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:15,840
So let's get into the dialogue again and we'll be back with Fluency Builder.

106
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:16,880
Why didn't you text me last night?

107
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:17,520
What?

108
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:19,300
I sent you three or four messages.

109
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:20,860
I didn't get any of them.

110
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,300
I was waiting for you to text me the address of where the party was and I never got your message.

111
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:25,720
Why didn't you just call?

112
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:27,780
I hate sending SMS messages.

113
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,260
Well, because I didn't have any credit on my phone.

114
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:31,280
I used it all up this month.

115
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:34,040
I thought you had an unlimited SMS plan.

116
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:34,760
I do.

117
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,780
I don't have any credit in my phone, it won't let me call or send messages.

118
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:39,640
No wonder you didn't get my texts.

119
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:44,800
All right, we've prepared three phrases for you.

120
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,480
Let's start with those now in Fluency Builder.

121
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:49,360
Fluency Builder.

122
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:52,670
So we're still talking about credit here.

123
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,310
Now when you have credit, you can use it.

124
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:00,050
But if you use all of it, we say you use it all up.

125
00:06:00,470 --> 00:06:00,650
Right.

126
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:02,160
We used it up.

127
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,390
To use something up.

128
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:05,850
So here, what does the up do?

129
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:11,530
Because we can use something, like I'm using my toothbrush, but to use up is different.

130
00:06:12,070 --> 00:06:14,610
Right, it means that you finished it.

131
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:17,640
It no longer exists.

132
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:18,650
So it's completed.

133
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:23,070
I had credit, I used it up, that means it is gone.

134
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:24,350
Right, exactly.

135
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:26,680
So for example, maybe paint.

136
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:30,450
You're painting a room and you used up all the white paint.

137
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,600
So you used all the paint, you have no more paint.

138
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,210
It's gone, so it's all used up.

139
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:39,110
It's a very important phrase to remember, it's used up.

140
00:06:39,510 --> 00:06:39,910
That's right.

141
00:06:40,410 --> 00:06:48,200
Now going back to SMS and messages, the question came up and said, I thought you had an unlimited SMS plan.

142
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,420
All right, so an unlimited SMS plan.

143
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:56,240
We know what SMS means, but first of all, let's talk about a plan.

144
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:00,950
Because when we talk about cell phones, it's important to remember that some people buy credit, that's how they get service.

145
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:02,770
But some people buy plans.

146
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,190
This is very popular, by the way, in America, this is how most phones are.

147
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:08,640
What is a plan?

148
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:12,470
So basically, it's just like any other service, like maybe internet.

149
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:17,510
You pay a monthly fee and you get a certain amount of this service.

150
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:24,890
So maybe you pay $50 and you get 100 minutes to use on your cell phone, an unlimited amount of SMS.

151
00:07:25,670 --> 00:07:38,960
All right, so basically, my cell phone provider, AT&T or Telecom, they will say, for this price, you get this much time, this many messages each month, and you have to keep paying it.

152
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:42,200
Normally, it's one year or two years that you have to commit.

153
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:44,440
You can't say, nah, I don't want this anymore.

154
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:52,760
But the important thing here is that the person bought an unlimited SMS plan.

155
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:56,120
So this keyword there, unlimited, means it has no limit.

156
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:59,520
It means you can use it as much as you want.

157
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:05,840
So unlimited, no limits, that means you can do it as much as you want.

158
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:13,540
You can have unlimited calls, you get unlimited SMS, you can have unlimited data, for example.

159
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:14,140
Exactly.

160
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:15,440
Very good.

161
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:19,540
And we end it with saying, well, no wonder you didn't get my text.

162
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:20,840
Well, because he didn't have credit.

163
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:22,440
No wonder you didn't get it.

164
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:30,980
I know wonder is a great and very important phrase, and it's something we say when we finally understand why something happened.

165
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:32,760
Right, we realize what happened.

166
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:37,660
So Marco, gosh, why didn't you respond to my calls?

167
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:39,760
Well, I didn't have any credit.

168
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:41,160
No wonder.

169
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:44,180
That means, oh, now I understand.

170
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:45,040
No wonder.

171
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:46,720
Exactly.

172
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:58,060
It's a great phrase to start off your sentence by explaining or letting the other person know that you finally understand why this happened or you understand what the situation was.

173
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:08,280
All right, so let's take a listen to the dialogue one last time.

174
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:09,360
Why didn't you text me last night?

175
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:09,980
What?

176
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:11,720
I sent you three or four messages.

177
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:13,340
I didn't get any of them.

178
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:16,760
I was waiting for you to text me the address of where the party was and I never got your message.

179
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,200
Why didn't you just call?

180
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,260
I hate sending SMS messages.

181
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:22,640
Well, because I didn't have any credit on my phone.

182
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:24,400
I used it all up this month.

183
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:26,500
I thought you had an unlimited SMS plan.

184
00:09:26,780 --> 00:09:30,220
I do, but if I don't have any credit in my phone, it won't let me call or send messages.

185
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:32,100
No wonder you didn't get my texts.

186
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:35,820
All right, we're back.

187
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:41,440
So talking about messages and everything, do you send a lot of messages?

188
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,960
Because I know some people who send like 100 messages a day.

189
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:45,160
Well, that's usually teenagers.

190
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:47,340
Yeah, I don't send 100 messages a day.

191
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,800
I mostly use messages.

192
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,320
I don't call people very often.

193
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:55,000
I find that messages are easier and cheaper, actually.

194
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:01,280
But I can't imagine sending a thousand messages a day or a hundred messages a day.

195
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:03,640
Your thumbs would get so tired.

196
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:10,880
Well, I remember, well, this is back in the day when you had the little Nokia phones and you didn't have the touch screens and all this stuff.

197
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:23,940
I did have some friends who were very, very agile with sending these messages and some did send, I don't know, 100, 200 messages a day because it was that new way of communicating with people.

198
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:24,660
Yeah, it's very exciting.

199
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:35,850
And I will also mention, and this is something that my parents complain about, there is something in America and I'm sure in England as well called text talk or text speak.

200
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:43,130
And so because messages are short, people have started to change the way they spell or write words.

201
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:50,030
So instead of saying F-O-R, this is for you, they write, this is number for you.

202
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:55,970
And it's so funny because you see these normal words but written in a strange new way.

203
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:05,040
And people who are older, I think my parents' age, and I do too to some extent, really don't like it because it's so different.

204
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:12,250
It's especially very popular with the younger generation because it translates now to all sorts of messaging.

205
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:19,960
So before, as you say, it was because of the limitations, the amount of characters you could send in a message.

206
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:23,400
But now it's just become something that people have gotten in the habit of doing.

207
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:30,810
So for example, instead of writing skate, like skateboard, they would write S-K and then the number eight.

208
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:32,380
So skate.

209
00:11:32,570 --> 00:11:33,190
That's right.

210
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:34,380
Skate.

211
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:35,380
Skate.

212
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:35,870
It's so strange.

213
00:11:36,470 --> 00:11:39,230
And I know it actually happens in other languages as well.

214
00:11:39,270 --> 00:11:39,870
Yeah, a lot.

215
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:42,280
Italian, they do it a lot.

216
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:43,250
And Spanish, I'm sure as well.

217
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:49,440
But we call it text speak because it's very, very short and kind of abbreviated.

218
00:11:49,440 --> 00:12:00,160
But it's a problem from my understanding is because these younger generations, they use it so much that they really kind of start forgetting the proper grammar and proper spelling.

219
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:10,320
So even you will find, I saw a report the other day, essays or reports handed in with these terrible spelling mistakes.

220
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:10,680
That's awful.

221
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:17,640
Well, I hope, hope, hope that people are learning grammar and spelling, especially non-native learners of English.

222
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:21,360
So if you're doing homework for your English class, don't use these.

223
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:22,620
But it might be interesting to look them up.

224
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:26,480
So let us know though, do you text message people?

225
00:12:26,680 --> 00:12:27,820
Do you send these text messages?

226
00:12:28,140 --> 00:12:29,760
Do you have SMS service in your country?

227
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:31,060
Let us know.

228
